{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_117911486#9_248580444","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports the idea that giving a product away for free can imply customers will find no value in it, but the document appears to be a broader pricing/freemium discussion rather than dedicated to explaining why free things have lower perceived value.","learned":"A useful mechanism is price/value signaling: if a product is free, customers may infer it has no value because otherwise the seller could charge for it."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"giving your product away for free\" \"no value\")","reason":"Search the exact promising wording to find the source or related articles focused on how free pricing signals no value."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"giving your product away for free\" \"no value\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_117911486#8_248578319","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3893","rank":1,"snippet":"life these days, you've got to get everything for free you can. - Carl Rogers photo credit: cobalt123 Carl here may be exaggerating a little bit, but his underlying message rings true. After all, who doesn’t love free stuff? This kind of thinking provides the foundation for the freemium model: Everyone loves free stuff, so let’s give the product away for free to attract users and then figure out a way how to get these users to pay. There’s a couple problems with this thinking. First, since you’re <cover>giving your product away for free, you’re implying that your customers will find no value </cover>from it (otherwise, you could charge something and they would be willing to pay). Second, and more importantly, you’re leaving money on the table because you're not collecting revenue from customers who were originally willing to pay for your product but are now using it for free. ( When was the last time you had a customer volunteer to pay you?).\" , \"start_char\": 3152.000000 , \"title\": \"6 Must Read Pricing Strategy Quotes\" , \"url\": \""}}
